Building aloha-oe
=================

You need a C11 compiler, make, and the development files for your
system's C library and audio interfaces.  GNU make and BSD make are
supported.  No configure script is needed.

From the source directory, run:

        make
        make check

The program is left in this directory.  It may be run here without
installation.  The check target examines the score; it does not open
an audio device.

The usual make variables may be set on the command line.  For example:

        make CC=clang CFLAGS='-g -O0 -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra'

CPPFLAGS, LDFLAGS, and LDLIBS are also available.  LDLIBS defaults to
-lm.  Run make clean before changing compilers or build options; make
does not record the options used to compile an object file.


System notes
============

The compiler's predefined macros select the playback code.  SYSTEM
selects additional linker options and defaults to the result of
uname -s.  Set it explicitly when cross-compiling.

macOS (Darwin)
        Install the Apple command-line development tools.  The program
        links with AudioToolbox and CoreFoundation.  Build and playback
        have been checked on macOS.

iOS (jailbroken)
        The macOS backend is used.  Before playback it selects the
        media playback audio session, so the ring/silent switch does
        not mute it.  Cross-compile with an iOS SDK and sign the result:

        make SYSTEM=Darwin \
             CC='clang -target arm64-apple-ios14.0 -isysroot /path/to/iPhoneOS.sdk'
        ldid -S aloha-oe

FreeBSD and DragonFly BSD
        The program uses the OSS interface in <sys/soundcard.h> and
        writes to /dev/dsp by default.

Linux
        The kernel ALSA headers, including <sound/asound.h>, must be
        installed.  The program opens /dev/snd/pcmC0D0p by default.
        It does not link with libasound or contact a sound server.

NetBSD and OpenBSD
        The program uses each system's <sys/audioio.h> interface.
        The default devices are /dev/audio and /dev/audio0,
        respectively.

Windows
        Use MinGW-w64, with its getopt_long implementation, from a
        shell providing make and the usual Unix commands.  MSVC is
        not supported by this makefile.  WinMM is the only additional
        Windows library.  For a typical cross build:

        make CC=x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc SYSTEM=Windows_NT \
             PROGRAM=aloha-oe.exe

        The link uses -municode for the wide-character entry point and
        -lwinmm for playback.  The default output is WAVE_MAPPER.

Other systems
        If the C library supplies the required interfaces, the null
        playback backend permits WAV output with --no-play --wav FILE.
        A new playback backend belongs in src/audio; see doc/PORTING.

Only macOS has been tested at run time.  The remaining playback
backends have been reviewed against their system interfaces.

Direct device access requires the appropriate permissions.  A sound
server or another application may already have the device open.  Use
--device to select a different device.  The Unix device backends
require stereo, signed 16-bit little-endian samples at the requested
rate; they reject a changed rate rather than alter the pitch.


Installation
============

The default prefix is /usr/local.  Installation copies the executable
to PREFIX/bin and the manual page to PREFIX/share/man/man6:

        make install

Set PREFIX to change both locations, or set BINDIR and MANDIR
separately.  MANDIR names the directory above man6.

For a package build, DESTDIR provides a staging directory:

        make install PREFIX=/usr DESTDIR=/tmp/aloha-package

This installs beneath /tmp/aloha-package/usr without changing paths
compiled into the program.  No data files are required at run time.

INSTALL, INSTALL_PROGRAM, and INSTALL_DATA may be overridden.  The
defaults are install, install -m 755, and install -m 644.  To remove an
installation, use make uninstall with the same path variables and
PROGRAM setting used to install it.  Empty directories are retained.


Source distributions
====================

        make dist

This makes aloha-oe.tar.gz from the files listed in MANIFEST, with a
single aloha-oe directory at its root.  The archive contains source and
documentation.  TAR, DISTNAME, and DISTFILE may be set on the make command
line.  The dist target needs mktemp and GNU tar or bsdtar.  Archives use
the ustar format with numeric ownership; owner names and extended file
attributes are omitted.

make clean removes the program and object files.  It leaves archives
and any audio files you have made alone.
